  {
   "captions_sha256": "cae5fa7edc01d0b78d0e3270ea26392dc7a6aa5235d9b21030a30398ddfe440f",
   "created_at": "2026-11-22T06:56:10Z",
   "description": "Why do firefighters set a forest on fire on purpose? At Fort Stewart, Georgia, a helicopter drops small ignition balls over the longleaf pines and more than 1,200 acres burn in about 45 minutes, a controlled prescribed burn. NASA's FireSense project turned this burn into an experiment: three aircraft, four sensors and the AVIRIS-3 imaging spectrometer tracked the fire while it was still burning.\n\nIn this episode:\n0:00 Ignition balls over Georgia\n0:19 Fort Stewart and prescribed fire\n0:58 NASA FireSense: the experiment\n1:20 The helicopter and the sphere dispenser\n1:50 Three planes, four sensors, AVIRIS-3\n2:26 Satellites vs aircraft\n2:42 Why these forests need fire\n\nSources:\n- NASA SVS, \"FireSense at Fort Stewart Prescribed Burn\" (produced video, Sept 2025): https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14894\n- NASA SVS, FireSense at Fort Stewart b-roll (April 2025): https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/14838\n\nFootage: NASA via the Scientific Visualization Studio (SVS 14838, 14894). Public domain; NASA does not endorse this video. The satellite fleet shot is a NASA animation; the AVIRIS-3 fire image is NASA JPL data. The acres counter and the satellite-vs-aircraft timeline are Orbital Atlas graphics (timeline illustrative).\nNarration: synthetic voice (ElevenLabs).\n\n#PrescribedBurn #NASA #Wildfire #FortStewart #OrbitalAtlas",
